hen: the critique stage tends to focus on style/issues and might lose sight of the original requirement\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:reads [:draft :critique :prompt] :writes [:revised-draft]}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** fixed 3-stage refinement burns 3x the tokens of a single pass — overkill when the output is short or the prompt is straightforward (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: output is under 200 tokens AND the prompt is concrete (no nuance) — single :llm with a careful instruction is enough\n    - Recommended fix: use a single :llm with an instruction that explicitly tells the model 'draft, then critique, then revise inline' — much cheaper and often comparable quality\n\n  - **Failure mode:** open-ended (unbounded N iterations until convergence) is the wrong tool here — that's validation-loop's job, not iterative-refinement's (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the loop needs to repeat until quality threshold is met OR the work needs explicit retry-on-fail semantics\n    - Recommended fix: use validation-loop (with a producer + a validator + a retry budget) for unbounded-quality scenarios; iterative-refinement is for fixed-N refinement passes\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - technical writing improvement: draft documentation → critique for clarity/missing-context → revise\n  - creative writing polish: draft scene → critique for pacing/voice → revise\n  - executive summary tightening: draft summary → critique for jargon/specificity → revise\n\n#### Alternative #1 — Iterative refinement (confidence: 0.95)\nWhy this fits: Matches the structural need for iterative refinement for a complex, multi-constraint physical training plan. As a fingerprint of the class above, it shares the same strengths for nuan